From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 18:29:40 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: msgbuf: keep the flowring tx count from going
 negative

Every Wi-Fi disconnect before system sleep logged
"brcmf_msgbuf_delete_flowring: timed out waiting for txstatus", one or
two per sleep cycle on BCM4387 Macs (M1 Pro, M2).

A kprobe on brcmf_msgbuf_delete_flowring() reading each flowring's
outstanding_tx showed two cases, 10 timeouts in 12 sleep cycles:

- the count is positive: frames were queued to the AP when the link
  went down, and the firmware completes them only when it flushes the
  ring for the delete request, so the wait before that request cannot
  succeed;
- the count is negative: after a timeout the count is set to zero,
  the late completions then take it below zero, and nothing resets it
  when the flowid is used again, so the next delete on that flowid
  waits out its full timeout as well.

Reset the count when a ring is created, never decrement it below zero,
and report the timeout at debug level with the count: it is expected
when the peer is gone, and the ring is deleted either way.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit 393fefb80fc518083beb4bccb5fcb8dd9800466f)
---
 .../broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c      | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
index 5806a0b140..b3b22d16be 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
@@ -729,6 +729,10 @@ brcmf_msgbuf_flowring_create_worker(struct brcmf_msgbuf *msgbuf,
 		return BRCMF_FLOWRING_INVALID_ID;
 	}
 
+	/* A new ring has nothing in flight, whatever the previous ring on
+	 * this flowid left behind.
+	 */
+	atomic_set(&msgbuf->flowrings[flowid]->outstanding_tx, 0);
 	brcmf_commonring_config(msgbuf->flowrings[flowid],
 				BRCMF_H2D_TXFLOWRING_MAX_ITEM,
 				BRCMF_H2D_TXFLOWRING_ITEMSIZE, dma_buf);
@@ -1043,7 +1047,13 @@ brcmf_msgbuf_process_txstatus(struct brcmf_msgbuf *msgbuf, void *buf)
 
 	set_bit(flowid, msgbuf->txstatus_done_map);
 	commonring = msgbuf->flowrings[flowid];
-	atomic_dec(&commonring->outstanding_tx);
+	/* brcmf_msgbuf_delete_flowring() zeroes the count when it stops
+	 * waiting, and the firmware completes those frames later, when it
+	 * flushes the ring. Don't let them take the count below zero: a
+	 * negative count outlives the ring, and the next delete on this
+	 * flowid then waits out its full timeout.
+	 */
+	atomic_dec_if_positive(&commonring->outstanding_tx);
 
 	ifp = brcmf_get_ifp(msgbuf->drvr, tx_status->msg.ifidx);
 	/* Key on the flowring's ifidx, which the host assigned when it queued
@@ -1610,7 +1620,12 @@ void brcmf_msgbuf_delete_flowring(struct brcmf_pub *drvr, u16 flowid)
 		retry--;
 	}
 	if (!retry) {
-		brcmf_err("timed out waiting for txstatus\n");
+		/* Expected when the peer is gone (disconnect, suspend): the
+		 * firmware completes these frames only when it flushes the
+		 * ring for the delete request below.
+		 */
+		brcmf_dbg(MSGBUF, "flowring %u: %d tx outstanding, deleting it anyway\n",
+			  flowid, atomic_read(&commonring_del->outstanding_tx));
 		atomic_set(&commonring_del->outstanding_tx, 0);
 	}
 
